Module 8: Using Tableau for Visualization and Creating a Dashboard

Overview
In this module, I learned how to use Tableau to visualize data and build interactive dashboards. 
Tableau is a powerful data visualization tool that allows analysts to transform raw data into meaningful visual insights such as charts, graphs, and dashboards.

This module focused on connecting data sources, creating visualizations, and combining those visualizations into dashboards to support data-driven decision making.


Key Concepts
Data Visualization: The graphical representation of data. It helps identify: Trends, Patterns, Outliers, Comparisons

Visualizations make complex data easier to understand.

Dimensions and Measures

Dimensions: Qualitative data. Used to categorize data. Examples: Country, Movie Title, Continent

Measures: Quantitative data. Numeric values that can be aggregated. Examples: Population, Revenue, Budget

Tableau Workspace Components

Data Pane: Displays available fields from the dataset

Rows and Columns Shelves: Control the layout of the visualization

Marks Card: Controls appearance, such as: Color, Size, Labels, Detail

Creating Visualizations: Visualizations are created by dragging and dropping fields into Rows and Columns.

Examples include: Bar charts, Line charts, Maps, Scatter plots. 

Tableau automatically generates visuals based on selected data.

Filters: Filters allow focusing on specific subsets of data.
Examples: Filter by country, Filter by year, Filter by category. Filters improve analysis accuracy.

Dashboards: A dashboard is a collection of multiple visualizations displayed together.
Dashboards allow users to: View multiple insights at once, Compare data across charts, Interact with filters and visuals, Communicate findings effectively
Dashboards are commonly used in business intelligence reporting.

